//? Title: Frozen random features and echo-state reservoirs
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
Fit linear readouts on frozen tanh features. Compare a static extreme
learning machine on a nonlinear regression problem with an echo-state
reservoir that must recover delays in a signal.
)

> Freeze the hidden weights and fit the readout

The static model applies a random nonlinear feature map independently to
each input. The recurrent model adds the previous hidden state after
multiplying it by a cyclic shift with gain 0.82. The shift has spectral
norm 0.82 and tanh is 1-Lipschitz, so changing the initial state has a
decaying effect under a common input sequence. The first 16 recurrent
states are discarded from the fit as a washout.

In both cases, \(X\) contains a column of ones and the hidden states.
The readout solves \((X^T X+\lambda D)w=X^T y\), with \(D\) diagonal
and zero for the intercept. Only the fitting rows contribute to the
system. Test states continue from the fitting sequence and use inputs
as they arrive. The targets for the delayed problem are never fed back.

> Fit both upstream fixtures

The static target is a nonlinear function of the current input. The
recurrent target combines two delays with an interaction of two other
lags. These are separate tasks, each with its own upstream seed and input
weights. Compare each model with its own zero-prediction baseline.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/reservoir_computing.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

int main() {
    namespace nn = dm::nn;
    namespace g = dm::graphics;
    constexpr int steps = 512, split = 340, hidden = 28;
    nn::set_num_threads(2);
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    auto plots = g::RLayout::create(2, 1).size(950, 560);
    for (const bool recurrent : {false, true}) {
        std::mt19937 rng(recurrent ? 50581 : 50582);
        std::normal_distribution<double> normal(0, recurrent ? .42 : 1.4);
        std::vector<double> weights(2 * hidden), cycle(hidden * hidden), signal(steps);
        for (auto& w : weights) w = normal(rng);
        for (int j = 0; j < hidden; ++j) cycle[j * hidden + (j + 1) % hidden] = .82;
        std::uniform_real_distribution<double> draw(-1, 1);
        for (auto& u : signal) u = draw(rng);
        const auto value = [&](int t) { return t >= 0 ? signal[t] : 0.; };
        std::vector<double> inputs(steps * 2), labels(steps);
        for (int t = 0; t < steps; ++t) {
            const double u = value(t);
            inputs[2 * t] = 1;
            inputs[2 * t + 1] = u;
            labels[t] = recurrent ? .75 * value(t-2) - .25 * value(t-4) + .15 * value(t-1) * value(t-3)
                                  : std::sin(3 * u) + .24 * u * u;
        }
        const auto design = nn::reservoir_features(
            nn::from_vector(inputs, {steps, 2}), nn::from_vector(weights, {2, hidden}),
            recurrent ? nn::from_vector(cycle, {hidden, hidden}) : nn::Tensor{});
        const auto target = nn::from_vector(labels, {steps, 1});
        const int start = recurrent ? 16 : 0;
        const auto coefficients = nn::fit_reservoir_readout(
            nn::slice_axis(design, 0, start, split), nn::slice_axis(target, 0, start, split), .01);
        const auto prediction = nn::matmul(nn::slice_axis(design, 0, split, steps), coefficients);
        const auto actual = nn::slice_axis(target, 0, split, steps);
        const double mse = nn::mean(nn::square(prediction - actual)).item();
        const double zero = nn::mean(nn::square(actual)).item();
        if (!(mse < .35 * zero)) throw std::runtime_error("Readout fails held-out source criterion");

        const std::string name = recurrent ? "echo_state" : "extreme_learning";
        nn::save_npz("build/" + name + ".npz",
            {{"features", design}, {"readout", coefficients}, {"prediction", prediction}});
        std::vector<double> times(steps - split);
        std::iota(times.begin(), times.end(), split);
        auto plot = g::RPlot::create().title(recurrent ? "Echo-state reservoir" : "Extreme learning machine")
            .x_label("Held-out time step").y_label("Response");
        plot.lines(times, actual.to_vector<double>(), "Target", {50, 70, 90})
            .lines(times, prediction.to_vector<double>(), "Prediction", {208, 87, 48});
        plots.add(plot);
        std::print("{} held-out MSE {:.9f}, zero baseline {:.9f}.\n\n", name, mse, zero);
    }
    plots.save_svg("build/reservoir_computing.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
extreme_learning held-out MSE 0.000014309, zero baseline 0.569209565.

echo_state held-out MSE 0.002126608, zero baseline 0.210767953.

\image(figures/reservoir_computing.svg)
// result_end

Both readouts solve the fitting problem without updating the hidden weights.
The chronological split and the zero baseline test useful predictions on
new inputs. Because the targets differ, the two MSE values do not measure
the effect of adding recurrence to the same task.
